, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =10+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-49176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "20380cbc6ad5d4379a0e4b52d5f4d78bd11572956f561f53b3159fe669d0fe08"} {"question" "q-49176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-49176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "10+\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "10+\\pi "] ["math" "5+\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "5+\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-49176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10+\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-316261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/assets/d36f2859865da58d226a738d8338f726df950db75b7421b604bb692c9049d54a.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-316261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,-2]$ and the line segment for $x\\in (-2,1].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-316261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "20380cbc6ad5d4379a0e4b52d5f4d78bd11572956f561f53b3159fe669d0fe08"} {"question" "q-316261" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-316261" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-338331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/assets/06ace831b877406e48fe8f1ab38a7244e7ade878d7c77b99d8230f40ad307207.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-338331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-6,-3],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-3,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,2],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (2,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}3(3) \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,2].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+2)(2) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (2,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}3(6) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =4.5+3+6+9 \\\\  & =22.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-338331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "20380cbc6ad5d4379a0e4b52d5f4d78bd11572956f561f53b3159fe669d0fe08"} {"question" "q-338331" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-338331" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{45}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-338336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/assets/19b94b149f752dc284f2cc002d786bc002621333a8eaadf2396e131640046c89.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-338336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,0].$   The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4)(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,3].$    The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+0)(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$   The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+2)(2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}-2 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-338336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "20380cbc6ad5d4379a0e4b52d5f4d78bd11572956f561f53b3159fe669d0fe08"} {"question" "q-338336" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-338336" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-339266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/assets/f418496b3460fddc13ea2beb4c099e97ed4d2fa1d89e04c01862d142e4d4177f.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-339266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-6,-3),$ the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in (-3,3),$ and the line segment for $x\\in (3,6].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-3).$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,3).$ The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle of radius $3$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh+\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =6(1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+6+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =18+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-339266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "20380cbc6ad5d4379a0e4b52d5f4d78bd11572956f561f53b3159fe669d0fe08"} {"question" "q-339266" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "6454a1ecf3aff5e370a6d216397d29d43f4030ac936a95a053a205fc6130b4f4"} {"question" "q-339266" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "18+\\frac{9\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065207/state.json" "file_sha256" "